You can customize the schema a dbt model builds into using **custom schemas**, which you configure in your `dbt_project.yml` file (or in the model file using a `config` block). By default, dbt builds models into the target schema you define, but this behavior can be changed through configurations [build\sql-models.md].

Specifically, the documentation notes: "Build models into separate [schemas](/docs/build/custom-schemas)" as one of the example configurations available for models [build\sql-models.md].

Additionally, in the context of your `profiles.yml` file, the `schema` field is used to define the default schema dbt builds models into, and it's required for dbt to work correctly with your project metadata (for example, it appears on the dbt docs page even if not present in the database) [local\connect-data-platform\singlestore-setup.md].

If you want to support concurrent development by prefixing table names with the schema, you can add a custom macro like this to your project:

```sql
-- macros/generate_alias_name.sql
{% macro generate_alias_name(custom_alias_name=none, node=none) -%}
    {%- if custom_alias_name is none -%}
        {{ node.schema }}__{{ node.name }}
    {%- else -%}
        {{ node.schema }}__{{ custom_alias_name | trim }}
    {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}
```

This macro takes the `schema` field from your `profiles.yml` and uses it as a table name prefix. For example, if you set `schema=dev` in your `.dbt/profiles.yml` file, running the `customers` model would create a table named `dev__customers` in your database [local\connect-data-platform\singlestore-setup.md].

The provided context does not include the specific detailed documentation page for `/docs/build/custom-schemas`, so I can't give the full details on all custom schema configuration options beyond what's referenced above.
